        {
          "path": "_m/w/s/q/q/old-taipei-railway-workshop-wp/category-2.jpg",
          "filename": "鐵道部臺北工場模型.jpg",
          "caption": "The building's exterior facade showcases a remarkable arrangement of brick arches. Its roof structure takes inspiration from the initial design of Taipei Railway Station, crafted by Liu Ming-Chuan with iron tracks imported from the West. Initially, the building boasted six continuous arches on both its eastern and western sides, serving as entryways and maintenance facilities for trains. However, as a consequence of the underground railway project and the expansion of Zhengzhou Road to accommodate the construction of the Civic Boulevard, the tower located on the building's northern side and two archways were removed. At present, the structure preserves only four archways as a testament to its architectural history.",
          "alt": "The building's exterior facade showcases a remarkable arrangement of brick arches. Its roof structure takes inspiration from the initial design of Taipei Railway Station, crafted by Liu Ming-Chuan with iron tracks imported from the West. Initially, the building boasted six continuous arches on both its eastern and western sides, serving as entryways and maintenance facilities for trains. However, as a consequence of the underground railway project and the expansion of Zhengzhou Road to accommodate the construction of the Civic Boulevard, the tower located on the building's northern side and two archways were removed. At present, the structure preserves only four archways as a testament to its architectural history.",
